<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Spartiates</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as homoioi, Spartiate</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Spartiates were the full citizens of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, also called homoioi (peers). They were trained from boyhood in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoge">agoge</a>, served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> and had to contribute to a common mess, living from land worked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>. Their number fell from about 8,000 in 480 BCE to fewer than 1,000 by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, a decline in manpower that was the main cause of Sparta's fall as a great power.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Classical accounts credited the Spartan constitution to the lawgiver Lycurgus, but the sources' dates for him are so inconsistent that it is impossible to say whether he was a historical figure. At some point in the late Archaic period Spartan society shifted from a monarchical system to an aristocracy of warriors. The change was probably linked to the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> warfare and to the conquest of Messenia, which provided both the basis of helotage and the need for a large force to hold down the conquered people. The Spartiates became a permanently armed master class living off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helot</a> labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Under the kleros system each full citizen was assigned land worked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>, and a certain income was needed to pay the dues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syssitia">syssitia</a>, the common messes, on which Spartiate status depended. Politically the Spartiates formed the army assembly, which elected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a>. Boys lived with their mothers until the age of 7 and then entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoge">agoge</a>, where surviving accounts record athletics, dances and reading and writing rather than weapons drill, but also deliberate hunger that drove the boys to steal. Some young men served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crypteia">crypteia</a>, which assassinated helots thought likely to lead revolts.</p><p>In battle the Spartiates fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> with the aspis, dory, xiphos and kopis, and stood out among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> for sophisticated drills: subdivided units with officers, marching in step to flutes, and wheeling and countermarching in formation. Losing one's shield was a mark of shame. The Skiritai served beside them as scouts and light infantry, and their cavalry, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippeis">hippeis</a>, was thought unimpressive by contemporaries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Spartiates who could no longer pay their mess dues probably fell into the Hypomeiones, the inferiors, and the strict qualifications made lost men hard to replace. The decline, known as oliganthropia, shows in the battles of the late 5th and early 4th centuries BCE: 5,000 Spartiates at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, 4,200 at Mantinea, 2,500 at Coronea and only 1,500 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a>. The loss of 300 Spartiates at Mantinea was hard felt, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> never fully recovered from that battle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Spartiate” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spartan society rested on a sharp division between a small body of full citizens, the <b>Spartiates</b> or homoioi, peers, the free but subordinate perioikoi, and the helots, state-owned serfs of Laconia and Messenia who worked the land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A man of <b>Spartiate</b> birth who passed through the state upbringing (the agoge) and paid his dues to a common mess became one of the homoioi, the “equals”; poverty or cowardice could cost him that place.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
